{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 7, :comments ["<b>כִּי לֹא אַצְדִּיק רָשָׁע.</b> According to the Sages, of blessed memory (Sanhedrin 33b), who explained that ״נָקִי״ — this is one who has an advocate arguing his merit, and ״צַדִּיק״ — this is one who emerged righteous in his judgment and has an advocate arguing his liability, the verse intends to state the reason for the matter, so that you should not say: How, after his verdict has been reached for liability, can the judgment revert to merit? Therefore it says <b>כִּי לֹא אַצְדִּיק רָשָׁע</b> — meaning: whichever way you look at it, if what emerged as liable is according to truth and justice — I will not justify the wicked, and the one arguing merit will be of no avail; and if what emerged as liable was not according to justice — why should the innocent be swallowed up for nothing. And corresponding to the case of one who emerged righteous in his judgment, it also says <b>כִּי לֹא אַצְדִּיק רָשָׁע</b> — meaning: since he emerged righteous, I did not justify him while he is wicked, for Elohim stands in the congregation of El and among Elohim He judges, and He judges with justice, and it is impossible that HaShem would seal a matter of falsehood, heaven forbid. Go out and learn what they said, of blessed memory (Ketubot 21b), that it is forbidden to seal a document that appears to be false. And if you say: Why do we not say the same when one emerged liable? You should know that HaShem relents concerning evil but does not relent concerning good, and one who emerged liable — HaShem relents to exonerate him; but one who emerged innocent — HaShem does not relent from the good. And sometimes this very distress suffices for a person — that his judgment emerged liable and terrors of death reach him when he sees that his verdict has been decided for death — and therefore afterward, among Eloheim He judges to exonerate him."], :book :exodus, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 23, :notes {0 "The closing phrase ״בִּרְאוֹתוֹ כִּי נִגְמַר דִּינוֹ לָמוּת״ is translated hyper-literally; the logic is that the terror itself serves as atonement, after which HaShem exonerates."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:32:49.230812169Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}